Autoimmune Hepatitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Fluctuating jaundice/fatigue, Co-occurring autoimmune diseases, Elevated IgG, Positive ASMA/ANA

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-005
Reason for Visit: Severe, overwhelming lethargy, new-onset dark urine, and migratory joint pain rendering the patient unable to complete ADLs.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, partner.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an insidious but currently severe manifestation of systemic malaise, visible fluctuating jaundice, and dark urine in a young patient. This profile indicated acute hepatocellular injury. Urgent home assessment was indicated to assess for signs of acute liver failure (encephalopathy, severe coagulopathy) that would demand immediate hospital admission, ensuring safety prior to the next available GP appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm so exhausted I can barely lift my head, my joints are aching constantly, and my urine looks like black tea."
History of Present Illness: Insidious onset of severe fatigue and systemic malaise over the last 4 weeks, sharply worsening over the past 3 days. Notes fluctuating jaundice. Reports migratory polyarthralgia affecting the small joints of the hands and wrists. Also reports oligomenorrhoea. Patient has a known history of type 1 diabetes.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound due to profound fatigue and joint pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Partner has taken leave from work to provide full-time care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically unwell, fatigued, but alert and oriented. No asterixis.
Physical Exam: Scleral icterus visible. Abdominal examination reveals hepatomegaly with a firm, tender liver edge extending 3 cm below the costal margin. Mild maculopapular rash on the torso. Joint exam shows mild tenderness in bilateral MCP joints without gross effusion. Diagnostic tetrad features (fluctuating jaundice/fatigue, co-occurring autoimmune diseases) are clinically evident; elevated IgG and positive ASMA/ANA are presumed pending serology.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of good home support. Blood glucose monitoring equipment visible and well-maintained.
Medication Reconciliation: Insulin regimen reviewed and correct.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autoimmune Hepatitis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute flare)
Differentials:

Viral Hepatitis (e.g., Hep B/C) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Insidious presentation with prominent prominent autoimmune features (polyarthralgia, existing T1DM) and lack of distinct viral prodrome or risk factors.

Drug-Induced Liver Injury (DILI) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of recent initiation of hepatotoxic medications, herbal supplements, or significant paracetamol use.

Primary Biliary Cholangitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent fatigue and parenchymal tenderness rather than intractable pruritus as the initial driving symptom.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (High care needs)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic symptoms of an acute inflammatory flare of autoimmune hepatitis, characterized by profound fatigue, hepatomegaly, jaundice, and concurrent autoimmune markers (T1DM, polyarthralgia). The acute worsening of dark urine and functional decline necessitated an immediate after-hours physical assessment to confidently rule out fulminant hepatic failure (encephalopathy, clinically apparent coagulopathy/bruising). Although stable enough to avoid emergency transport, the clinical picture requires urgent, expedited diagnostic workup (LFTs, IgG, ASMA/ANA) and subsequent immunosuppressive therapy, which could not be safely delayed over an extended weekend period without risking severe, irreversible hepatocellular necrosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Urgent pathology request generated (LFTs, Coagulation profile, U&E, FBC, ANA, ASMA, LKM-1, IgG levels, viral hepatitis screen).
Positive Behavioral Change: Maintain strict hydration by drinking 200ml of water every hour while awake. This ensures your kidneys can effectively flush out the excess bilirubin causing the dark urine, preventing secondary kidney strain during this acute liver flare.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 24-48 hours to review urgent blood results and initiate specialist gastroenterology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes confused, excessively drowsy, develops a flapping tremor in the hands, or exhibits uncontrolled vomiting, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis requires blood tests and potentially a liver biopsy for confirmation.
Clinical Course: Without treatment, autoimmune damage to the liver progresses. Corticosteroid therapy usually induces rapid remission once the diagnosis is confirmed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor mental clarity and bruising. If you start bruising easily, vomit blood, or if your partner notices you becoming confused or difficult to wake, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system, which already attacks your pancreas causing diabetes, is currently attacking your liver cells. The blood tests will confirm this. Treatment will involve medications that temporarily dampen your immune system to stop the damage.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely in bed and avoid all over-the-counter medications, particularly paracetamol and NSAIDs. Your liver is acutely inflamed and cannot metabolize these drugs safely, which could rapidly precipitate acute liver failure.

